    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_d6ffbfdea02d25e730f032076c6cf1e3c1fb1f362c0f1953a9ef27de4062bc7b",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Deligne's purity strategy shows a proved case in which spectral realization is not enough: placing one variety in a family exposes monodromy, tensor constructions amplify possible violations, and positivity forces the modulus constraint. Analytic L-function families reuse the broader move from an isolated object to structured variation, but not Deligne's specific cohomological machinery.",
