    {
      "object_id": "mathia_synthesis_e2fa654357d345f66be3137847863b48d513ae2d10136a3d1c4117d8bf2e0c9a",
      "object_role": "synthesis",
      "proposed_content": "Amplification and resonance are distinct targeted-weight methods with a reusable common move. An amplifier inserts a short target-matched coefficient polynomial into a positive family moment to extract a pointwise bound; a resonator optimizes a multiplicative quadratic quotient, admits a universal multiplicative majorant in its finite regime, and can use a Mobius sign twist to change the selected extremal direction while retaining coefficient magnitudes.",
